1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,032
Before alarm clocks, somebody was paid to
wake you up.

2
00:00:04,072 --> 00:00:08,420
Before toilets, somebody was paid to empty
what was under you.

3
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:12,879
History is full of jobs that make your job
look like a holiday.

4
00:00:13,569 --> 00:00:16,649
Today: ten of the worst jobs in history,

5
00:00:16,689 --> 00:00:19,613
counted down from bad to unbelievable.

6
00:00:19,653 --> 00:00:23,825
Every one of them was real, somebody did
it every day,

7
00:00:23,865 --> 00:00:26,945
and somebody's mother was proud of them.

8
00:00:26,985 --> 00:00:27,647
Probably.

9
00:00:28,337 --> 00:00:29,105
Number ten.

10
00:00:29,145 --> 00:00:30,426
The knocker-upper.

11
00:00:30,466 --> 00:00:34,682
In industrial Britain, factory shifts
started before dawn,

12
00:00:34,722 --> 00:00:37,545
and almost nobody owned an alarm clock.

13
00:00:37,585 --> 00:00:43,196
So you walked the dark streets with a long
pole and tapped on bedroom windows

14
00:00:43,236 --> 00:00:45,031
until someone waved back.

15
00:00:45,721 --> 00:00:49,383
Some knocker-uppers used a pea shooter
instead,

16
00:00:49,423 --> 00:00:52,061
and fired dried peas at the glass.

17
00:00:52,101 --> 00:00:55,290
The pay was a few pence a week per house.

18
00:00:55,330 --> 00:00:58,834
And the trade lasted longer than you'd
think:

19
00:00:58,874 --> 00:01:04,190
the last ones are thought to have retired
in the nineteen seventies.

20
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:05,795
Number nine.

21
00:01:05,835 --> 00:01:07,387
The leech collector.

22
00:01:07,427 --> 00:01:12,879
In the eighteen hundreds, doctors used
leeches for almost everything.

23
00:01:12,919 --> 00:01:15,028
Somebody had to catch them.

24
00:01:15,068 --> 00:01:16,779
The method was simple.

25
00:01:16,819 --> 00:01:22,510
You waded into a cold marsh with bare
legs, and waited for them to bite.

26
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:28,438
A good collector could gather up to two
thousand five hundred leeches in a

27
00:01:28,478 --> 00:01:28,723
day.

28
00:01:28,763 --> 00:01:30,649
At the height of the craze,

29
00:01:30,689 --> 00:01:35,785
France alone imported more than thirty
million leeches in a single year.

30
00:01:35,825 --> 00:01:39,779
Many collectors suffered from blood loss
and infections.

31
00:01:40,469 --> 00:01:41,410
Number eight.

32
00:01:41,450 --> 00:01:42,316
The mudlark.

33
00:01:42,356 --> 00:01:45,636
In Victorian London, when the tide went
out,

34
00:01:45,676 --> 00:01:51,221
children walked into the stinking mud of
the River Thames, often barefoot,

35
00:01:51,261 --> 00:01:54,013
looking for anything they could sell.

36
00:01:54,703 --> 00:01:59,795
Lumps of coal, old rope, bones, copper
nails dropped from ships.

37
00:01:59,835 --> 00:02:02,682
A good day might earn a few pennies.

38
00:02:02,722 --> 00:02:05,408
The river was also London's sewer.

39
00:02:05,448 --> 00:02:10,139
The mudlarks went in anyway, every low
tide, in winter too.

40
00:02:10,829 --> 00:02:11,848
Number seven.

41
00:02:11,888 --> 00:02:13,151
The pure finder.

42
00:02:13,191 --> 00:02:18,688
Victorian tanneries needed something
special to soften fine leather,

43
00:02:18,728 --> 00:02:22,516
like the skins used for gloves and book
covers.

44
00:02:22,556 --> 00:02:24,715
That something was dog poo.

45
00:02:24,755 --> 00:02:26,343
They called it pure.

46
00:02:27,033 --> 00:02:32,191
So the pure finders walked the streets
with a bucket and a glove,

47
00:02:32,231 --> 00:02:35,389
sometimes only one glove, collecting it.

48
00:02:35,429 --> 00:02:39,308
Then they sold it to the tanneries by the
bucket.

49
00:02:39,348 --> 00:02:45,145
Somewhere, a very elegant pair of gloves
started its life in that bucket.

50
00:02:45,835 --> 00:02:46,632
Number six.

51
00:02:46,672 --> 00:02:47,925
The whipping boy.

52
00:02:47,965 --> 00:02:53,784
The story goes like this: a young prince
could not be beaten by his teachers,

53
00:02:53,824 --> 00:02:59,262
so when he misbehaved, his friend was
punished instead, in front of him.

54
00:02:59,952 --> 00:03:03,148
It's one of the most famous jobs in
history.

55
00:03:03,188 --> 00:03:04,986
There's just one problem.

56
00:03:05,026 --> 00:03:09,914
Historians have found very little evidence
that it really happened.

57
00:03:09,954 --> 00:03:14,547
The worst job on this list might be the
one that never existed.

58
00:03:14,587 --> 00:03:17,636
Which is honestly the best way to have it.

59
00:03:18,326 --> 00:03:19,209
Number five.

60
00:03:19,249 --> 00:03:20,518
The climbing boy.

61
00:03:20,558 --> 00:03:26,289
Chimneys were narrow, so chimney sweeps
sent small children up inside them,

62
00:03:26,329 --> 00:03:31,753
to scrape the soot off with their hands
and a brush, in total darkness.

63
00:03:32,443 --> 00:03:38,645
A law in seventeen eighty-eight said they
had to be at least eight years old.

64
00:03:38,685 --> 00:03:40,509
It was hardly enforced.

65
00:03:40,549 --> 00:03:43,103
Boys got stuck, burned and sick.

66
00:03:43,143 --> 00:03:48,533
It took until eighteen seventy-five for
Britain to finally stop it,

67
00:03:48,573 --> 00:03:52,019
by making every sweep get a police
licence.

68
00:03:52,709 --> 00:03:53,609
Number four.

69
00:03:53,649 --> 00:03:54,864
The gong farmer.

70
00:03:54,904 --> 00:03:59,724
In Tudor England, toilets emptied into
deep pits called gongs.

71
00:03:59,764 --> 00:04:05,603
When a pit was full, a gong farmer climbed
in and shoveled it out, by hand,

72
00:04:05,643 --> 00:04:06,622
into barrels.

73
00:04:07,312 --> 00:04:12,879
Because the job was so disgusting, they
were only allowed to work at night.

74
00:04:12,919 --> 00:04:18,262
One record says gong farmers at a royal
palace were paid sixpence a day,

75
00:04:18,302 --> 00:04:20,505
which was actually good money.

76
00:04:20,545 --> 00:04:23,570
Nobody wanted to know how they earned it.

77
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:25,213
Number three.

78
00:04:25,253 --> 00:04:26,283
The sin eater.

79
00:04:26,323 --> 00:04:32,090
In parts of Wales and western England,
folklore says that when someone died,

80
00:04:32,130 --> 00:04:35,070
bread and salt were placed on the body.

81
00:04:35,110 --> 00:04:40,572
A poor stranger was paid to eat it, and
with it, the dead person's sins.

82
00:04:41,262 --> 00:04:44,117
Afterwards, the sin eater was shunned,

83
00:04:44,157 --> 00:04:47,470
because he now carried everyone else's
sins.

84
00:04:47,510 --> 00:04:51,737
Historians still argue about how common
this really was.

85
00:04:51,777 --> 00:04:54,785
But it was written down, more than once.

86
00:04:54,825 --> 00:04:57,986
Imagine putting that on a job application.

87
00:04:58,676 --> 00:04:59,506
Number two.

88
00:04:59,546 --> 00:05:01,325
The Groom of the Stool.

89
00:05:01,365 --> 00:05:03,303
This one was a royal job.

90
00:05:03,343 --> 00:05:06,943
The groom helped the Tudor king on the
toilet.

91
00:05:06,983 --> 00:05:07,734
Every day.

92
00:05:07,774 --> 00:05:08,604
Personally.

93
00:05:09,294 --> 00:05:10,854
And here's the twist.

94
00:05:10,894 --> 00:05:14,360
It was one of the most powerful jobs at
court.

95
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:17,561
The groom was the man closest to the king,

96
00:05:17,601 --> 00:05:21,219
and became something like his private
secretary.

97
00:05:21,259 --> 00:05:25,562
The worst job on paper was the one every
nobleman wanted.

98
00:05:26,252 --> 00:05:27,102
Number one.

99
00:05:27,142 --> 00:05:28,800
The resurrection man.

100
00:05:28,840 --> 00:05:31,954
In the seventeen and eighteen hundreds,

101
00:05:31,994 --> 00:05:38,181
medical schools needed bodies to study,
and there were not enough legal ones.

102
00:05:38,221 --> 00:05:44,003
So, at night, men dug up fresh graves and
sold the bodies to anatomists.

103
00:05:44,693 --> 00:05:50,153
In eighteen twenty-eight, a body could
sell for around eight guineas,

104
00:05:50,193 --> 00:05:53,102
more than a laborer earned in months.

105
00:05:53,142 --> 00:05:56,291
Families guarded graves with iron cages.

106
00:05:56,331 --> 00:06:01,870
The trade only faded after the Anatomy Act
of eighteen thirty-two gave

107
00:06:01,910 --> 00:06:04,262
doctors legal bodies to study.

108
00:06:04,952 --> 00:06:09,007
And a bonus, because history wasn't all
terrible.

109
00:06:09,047 --> 00:06:13,352
In Cuban cigar factories, from the
eighteen sixties,

110
00:06:13,392 --> 00:06:19,286
the workers paid a man to sit on a
platform and read to them while they

111
00:06:19,326 --> 00:06:23,632
rolled cigars: newspapers, novels, even
Shakespeare.

112
00:06:24,322 --> 00:06:26,185
He was called the lector.

113
00:06:26,225 --> 00:06:31,135
The workers chose the books, and paid him
out of their own wages.

114
00:06:31,175 --> 00:06:35,779
Some cigars are even named after the
novels the lectors read.

115
00:06:35,819 --> 00:06:38,521
In Cuba, the job still exists today.

116
00:06:39,211 --> 00:06:42,039
So what do these jobs have in common?

117
00:06:42,079 --> 00:06:46,534
Each one existed because somebody needed a
problem solved,

118
00:06:46,574 --> 00:06:49,480
and there was no machine to do it yet.

119
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:50,643
No alarm clock.

120
00:06:50,683 --> 00:06:51,418
No sewers.

121
00:06:51,458 --> 00:06:52,348
No medicine.

122
00:06:52,388 --> 00:06:54,828
Just a person, willing to do it.

123
00:06:55,518 --> 00:07:00,267
And for most of them, that person was
poor, young, or both.

124
00:07:00,307 --> 00:07:03,758
The next time your job feels bad,
remember:

125
00:07:03,798 --> 00:07:09,602
at least nobody asked you to wade into a
marsh and wait for the leeches.

126
00:07:10,292 --> 00:07:13,645
Which of these would you take, if you had
to?

127
00:07:13,685 --> 00:07:15,455
Tell us in the comments.

128
00:07:15,495 --> 00:07:17,943
And if you liked this, subscribe.

129
00:07:17,983 --> 00:07:18,697
Next time,

130
00:07:18,737 --> 00:07:24,050
we'll look at the jobs from our own
century that history will laugh at.

